Keep the chat still while scrolling up through older messages.

The transcript is virtualized: a message it hasn't drawn yet counts as
280px tall, and only gets its real height once it's drawn, which happens as
the reader scrolls up to it. react-virtual then corrects the scroll offset
by the difference, mid-scroll. On iOS it defers that correction until the
fling ends, so the messages on screen jump by hundreds of pixels each time
one above them is measured; a page of older messages is 20 unmeasured ones.
Elsewhere a message drawn at its estimate overlaps the ones below it for a
frame, and near the bottom the browser clamps the corrected offset to the
list's old height, so the reader loses the rest of it.

Messages are now measured before the reader gets to them. A page of older
messages is drawn whole in the commit that lands it, so the existing hold
on the reader's position works from real heights; the rest are drawn a few
at a time while the pane is still (no scroll, touch, wheel, key or pointer
press in the last 150 ms), nearest the window first, and dropped again once
measured. And the list grows to its new height before an offset correction,
so it's never clamped.

Upstream bug: delete once upstream's VirtualBubbleList
(web/src/components/chat/Transcript.tsx) measures rows before they scroll
into the window, or react-virtual no longer moves the offset mid-scroll.

